<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<cfsSimulation xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
               xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation ../../../../../share/xml/CFS-Simulation/CFS.xsd"
               xmlns="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation">

  <documentation>
    <title>Coulomb friction with large sliding on triangular (tet-derived) contact facets</title>
    <authors><author>kroppert</author></authors>
    <date>2026-08-08</date>
    <keywords><keyword>contact</keyword></keywords>
    <references>none</references>
    <isVerified>no</isVerified>
    <description>
      ContactFrictionSlip3d on the tet mesh of ContactPatchTestTria3d: two stacked cubes
      Kuhn-split into TET4 at 2x2x2 and 3x3x3, so both contact surfaces are TRIA3 and the two
      interface triangulations do not match. The lower body is fixed at the bottom, 1e6 Pa
      presses on the top, and the secondary interface is dragged diagonally (u_x = u_y = 1e-3),
      so the slip direction is (1,1,0)/sqrt(2).

      The lower body is 20% WIDER than the upper one, so the primary surface overhangs the
      secondary and no secondary point can slide off it. That makes numPoints =
      numProjectedPoints = numActivePoints = 168 an exact discrete invariant of the converged
      state -- nothing slid off, nothing opened -- a far sharper thing to guard than a tolerance
      on a force.

      slidingType="large" on purpose: it re-runs the closest-point projection and the mortar
      re-cut on a TRIANGULAR reference domain on the deformed surface, rather than once on the
      undeformed one. Together with ContactPatchTestTria3d it covers all three shape-dependent
      functions of ContactInterface (IsInsideElement, ClampToElement, GetReferencePolygon) on
      ST_TRIA; the two integrators are shape-agnostic.

      What is asserted are the RESULTANTS, which equilibrium and the Coulomb law fix
      independently of the mesh: totalContactForce = 4e6 N and totalTangentialForce = mu * F_N =
      1.6e6 N. The pressure DISTRIBUTION is deliberately not asserted: with the sides left free
      the clamped bottom fights Poisson expansion, so the exact solution is genuinely
      non-uniform.
    </description>
  </documentation>

  <fileFormats>
    <input>
      <hdf5 fileName="ContactFrictionSlipTria3d.h5" linearizeEntities="__none__"/>
    </input>
    <output><hdf5/></output>
    <materialData file="mat.xml" format="xml"/>
  </fileFormats>

  <domain geometryType="3d">
    <regionList>
      <region name="master_elem" material="Aluminium"/>
      <region name="slave_elem" material="Aluminium"/>
    </regionList>
    <surfRegionList>
      <surfRegion name="master_iface"/>
      <surfRegion name="slave_iface"/>
      <surfRegion name="top"/>
      <surfRegion name="bottom"/>
    </surfRegionList>
  </domain>

  <fePolynomialList>
    <Lagrange><gridOrder/></Lagrange>
  </fePolynomialList>

  <integrationSchemeList>
    <scheme>
      <method>Gauss</method>
      <order>4</order>
      <mode>absolute</mode>
    </scheme>
  </integrationSchemeList>

  <sequenceStep>
    <analysis><static/></analysis>

    <pdeList>
      <mechanic subType="3d">
        <regionList>
          <region name="master_elem"/>
          <region name="slave_elem"/>
        </regionList>

        <contactList>
          <!-- normalPenalty=5, not the 100 the other contact tests use. Under LARGE sliding
               on a refined interface the active set chatters before it settles, and the
               higher the penalty the longer the chatter: measured on this model, 100 -> 13
               Newton iterations with a sequence that never repeats twice in a row, 50 -> 16,
               20 -> 14, 5 -> the set is final from iteration 3 on. The converged resultants
               are identical at every one of those penalties, so this costs nothing but
               penetration and buys a stable activeSetHistory to compare. The same chatter
               appears on an equivalent non-matching HEXA27/QUAD9 mesh, so it is a property of
               large sliding on a refined interface, not of the triangles. -->
          <contactPair name="cube_contact"
                       primarySurface="master_iface"
                       secondarySurface="slave_iface"
                       slidingType="large"
                       normalPenalty="5"
                       initialGapHandling="fromMesh"
                       integrationOrder="2">
            <friction type="coulomb" mu="0.4" tangentialPenalty="100"/>
          </contactPair>
        </contactList>

        <bcsAndLoads>
          <!-- lower body fully supported -->
          <fix name="bottom">
            <comp dof="x"/><comp dof="y"/><comp dof="z"/>
          </fix>
          <!-- Drag the interface diagonally. Both in-plane components are prescribed, so the
               slip direction is (1,1,0)/sqrt(2) and the traction must follow it. z is left
               free: the normal support is still the contact interface, which keeps
               totalContactForce an equilibrium statement. -->
          <displacement name="slave_iface">
            <comp dof="x" value="1e-3"/>
            <comp dof="y" value="1e-3"/>
          </displacement>
          <pressure name="top" value="1e6"/>
        </bcsAndLoads>

        <storeResults>
          <nodeResult type="mechDisplacement"><allRegions/></nodeResult>
        </storeResults>
      </mechanic>
    </pdeList>

    <linearSystems>
      <system>
        <solutionStrategy>
          <standard>
            <setup idbcHandling="penalty"/>
            <matrix storage="sparseNonSym" reordering="noReordering"/>
            <nonLinear method="newton" logging="no">
              <lineSearch type="none"/>
              <!-- Increment-based convergence, as in ContactFrictionSlip3d: the residual is
                   normalised by the LINEAR right-hand side only, while the friction force
                   and the penalty reaction to the prescribed sliding both sit in the
                   solution-dependent one. Pre-existing openCFS behaviour. -->
              <incStopCrit>1e-9</incStopCrit>
              <resStopCrit>1</resStopCrit>
              <maxNumIters>30</maxNumIters>
            </nonLinear>
          </standard>
        </solutionStrategy>
        <solverList><directLU/></solverList>
      </system>
    </linearSystems>

  </sequenceStep>
</cfsSimulation>
